  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"114\" data-end=\"333\"><strong data-start=\"114\" data-end=\"134\">Direct Bilirubin</strong> (also called <strong data-start=\"148\" data-end=\"172\">conjugated bilirubin</strong>) measures the amount of bilirubin that has been processed by the liver and is water-soluble. This test is part of the <strong data-start=\"291\" data-end=\"315\">liver function panel</strong> and helps assess:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"335\" data-end=\"445\">\n<li data-start=\"335\" data-end=\"366\">\n<p data-start=\"337\" data-end=\"366\"><strong data-start=\"337\" data-end=\"366\">Liver function and health</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"367\" data-end=\"391\">\n<p data-start=\"369\" data-end=\"391\"><strong data-start=\"369\" data-end=\"391\">Causes of jaundice</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"392\" data-end=\"445\">\n<p data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"445\"><strong data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"445\">Bile duct obstruction or hepatobiliary diseases</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"445\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</strong></p>\n<p>An <strong data-start=\"583\" data-end=\"612\">elevated direct bilirubin</strong> level typically indicates problems with the <strong data-start=\"657\" data-end=\"694\">excretion of conjugated bilirubin</strong> from the liver into the bile or intestines. Causes include:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Obstructive jaundice</strong></td>\n<td>Blockage in bile ducts due to gallstones, tumors, or strictures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hepatitis (acute or chronic)</strong></td>\n<td>Liver cell inflammation impairs bilirubin processing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cholestasis</strong></td>\n<td>Impaired bile flow from liver to intestines (intra- or extra-hepatic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Primary biliary cirrhosis or sclerosing cholangitis</strong></td>\n<td>Autoimmune or inflammatory bile duct damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dubin-Johnson or Rotor syndrome</strong></td>\n<td>Rare genetic disorders affecting bilirubin transport</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
